1
00:00:00,350 --> 00:00:05,114
Picture yourself a week after finishing Section 6, pleased with an agent that

2
00:00:05,114 --> 00:00:05,981
already works.

3
00:00:05,981 --> 00:00:11,054
That is exactly the moment this story happens in, because it is exactly the moment

4
00:00:11,054 --> 00:00:13,034
you would be least on guard too.

5
00:00:13,034 --> 00:00:15,819
The follow-up chaser worked exactly as built.

6
00:00:15,819 --> 00:00:21,016
Priya, being sensible, noticed it would sometimes draft a chase for a client who had

7
00:00:21,016 --> 00:00:22,811
already replied that morning.

8
00:00:22,811 --> 00:00:27,390
Her fix seemed obvious: give it a tool to search her mail first and check.

9
00:00:27,390 --> 00:00:28,565
Was the idea wrong?

10
00:00:28,565 --> 00:00:33,763
No, and that matters: Section 6.4 told you this exact improvement was coming, and it

11
00:00:33,763 --> 00:00:34,629
is a good one.

12
00:00:34,629 --> 00:00:39,826
The redundant drafts were a real annoyance, the fix was a known tool one plus button

13
00:00:39,826 --> 00:00:44,777
away, and the whole change was two minutes on a Thursday afternoon between other

14
00:00:44,777 --> 00:00:45,086
work.

15
00:00:45,086 --> 00:00:50,098
Every condition from lecture 8.2 was present: small effort, immediate benefit, an

16
00:00:50,098 --> 00:00:54,367
agent with weeks of earned trust, and no sense of beginning anything.

17
00:00:54,367 --> 00:00:56,285
So the worksheet stayed closed.

18
00:00:56,285 --> 00:01:01,483
Not decided against, just never quite risen to the level of a decision, which is how

19
00:01:01,483 --> 00:01:03,525
the worksheet is usually skipped.

20
00:01:03,525 --> 00:01:05,257
Nobody chooses recklessness.

21
00:01:05,257 --> 00:01:06,557
They choose Thursday.

22
00:01:07,107 --> 00:01:12,033
This is the exact gap Section 8.2 named: a small-feeling addition, skipped past the

23
00:01:12,033 --> 00:01:15,892
worksheet, carrying access wider than the job it was meant to do.

24
00:01:15,892 --> 00:01:19,928
Reconstruct the consent-screen moment, because you have stood in it.

25
00:01:19,928 --> 00:01:24,736
The tool needs a credential, the screen appears mid-flow, and it offers what such

26
00:01:24,736 --> 00:01:29,544
screens offer: the standard grant, phrased reasonably, with the narrower option a

27
00:01:29,544 --> 00:01:32,037
link or a dropdown away if you go looking.

28
00:01:32,037 --> 00:01:36,726
Priya was not reading a contract that afternoon, she was finishing a two minute

29
00:01:36,726 --> 00:01:41,712
change, and clicking agree on a screen is the most practised reflex in modern office

30
00:01:41,712 --> 00:01:42,009
life.

31
00:01:42,009 --> 00:01:46,935
She connected her own personal Gmail credential, authorised for everything, and the

32
00:01:46,935 --> 00:01:48,894
workflow lit up green and worked.

33
00:01:48,894 --> 00:01:50,081
Note that last part.

34
00:01:50,081 --> 00:01:52,337
Nothing about over-broad access fails.

35
00:01:52,337 --> 00:01:56,551
It succeeds, silently, holding more keys than its job needs, and waits.

36
00:01:57,101 --> 00:02:01,858
The agent did exactly what a search tool does: it looked across everything the

37
00:02:01,858 --> 00:02:06,555
credential could reach, found a thread that happened to mention Brightwell in

38
00:02:06,555 --> 00:02:11,556
passing, in an entirely different, confidential context, and pulled a line from it

39
00:02:11,556 --> 00:02:13,385
into the draft it was writing.

40
00:02:13,385 --> 00:02:16,923
Understand the mechanism, because nothing in it is exotic.

41
00:02:16,923 --> 00:02:21,680
A search tool ranks by relevance, and relevance has no concept of propriety: a

42
00:02:21,680 --> 00:02:26,437
Legal-labelled thread that mentions Brightwell is, to a search index, simply a

43
00:02:26,437 --> 00:02:28,876
strong match for the query "Brightwell".

44
00:02:28,876 --> 00:02:33,816
The label that told every human at Calloway and Finch "not for general eyes" told

45
00:02:33,816 --> 00:02:37,781
the tool nothing, because labels only bind things that read them.

46
00:02:37,781 --> 00:02:42,843
And the model, handed a search result by its own tool, treated it the way it treats

47
00:02:42,843 --> 00:02:45,099
everything that arrives: as material.

48
00:02:45,099 --> 00:02:50,039
Recall lecture 1.3's line, an agent's input is also its instructions, and here is

49
00:02:50,039 --> 00:02:54,918
its gentler sibling: an agent's input is also its source material, whatever that

50
00:02:54,918 --> 00:02:56,260
input turns out to be.

51
00:02:56,260 --> 00:02:57,602
Nothing malfunctioned.

52
00:02:57,602 --> 00:03:02,542
Wide access plus honest machinery produced exactly what it produces, which is the

53
00:03:02,542 --> 00:03:04,737
point of never granting wide access.

54
00:03:05,287 --> 00:03:10,129
Read that again: the tool's scope was wrong, and the agent still could not cause

55
00:03:10,129 --> 00:03:15,092
harm, because the one guardrail that mattered most was never the tool's permission

56
00:03:15,092 --> 00:03:15,455
level.

57
00:03:15,455 --> 00:03:20,418
It was the fact that nothing this agent produces leaves without a human reading it

58
00:03:20,418 --> 00:03:20,781
first.

59
00:03:20,781 --> 00:03:25,865
Ask yourself, right now, whether that same sentence would be true of every agent you

60
00:03:25,865 --> 00:03:26,954
have built so far.

61
00:03:26,954 --> 00:03:31,856
And run the counterfactual honestly, because in a compliance-heavy business it is

62
00:03:31,856 --> 00:03:32,461
not small.

63
00:03:32,461 --> 00:03:37,485
Had the chaser been allowed to send, a client contact would have received an email,

64
00:03:37,485 --> 00:03:42,508
under Priya's name, quoting from a privileged thread about a dispute he had no part

65
00:03:42,508 --> 00:03:42,690
in.

66
00:03:42,690 --> 00:03:47,713
At minimum, an excruciating apology and a question nobody wants from a client: what

67
00:03:47,713 --> 00:03:49,831
else can your systems see about me?

68
00:03:49,831 --> 00:03:53,523
In an industry that runs on confidentiality, plausibly worse.

69
00:03:53,523 --> 00:03:58,304
The distance between that paragraph and "she deleted a draft" is two words in a

70
00:03:58,304 --> 00:04:03,025
dropdown, chosen five weeks earlier, on a calm day, before anyone was tempted.

71
00:04:03,575 --> 00:04:08,462
Walk down the right hand column with your own agents in mind, and ask where your

72
00:04:08,462 --> 00:04:09,439
equivalents are.

73
00:04:09,439 --> 00:04:14,448
The first four rows are where things went wrong, and notice how quietly: no error,

74
00:04:14,448 --> 00:04:17,503
no red box, no alert you could have subscribed to.

75
00:04:17,503 --> 00:04:22,451
The last two rows are the entire defence, and you built both of them in Section 6

76
00:04:22,451 --> 00:04:24,101
without knowing this story.

77
00:04:24,101 --> 00:04:28,865
When you design your next agent, this table is the exercise worth repeating on

78
00:04:28,865 --> 00:04:33,875
paper: walk the worst Thursday you can imagine, row by row, and make sure at least

79
00:04:33,875 --> 00:04:37,723
two rows near the bottom hold without help from the rows above.

80
00:04:38,273 --> 00:04:43,320
Notice what she did not have to do: apologise to a client, explain a leaked detail,

81
00:04:43,320 --> 00:04:46,360
or rebuild trust with anyone outside her own desk.

82
00:04:46,360 --> 00:04:51,407
That is what "blast radius, not disaster" actually looks like when a guardrail does

83
00:04:51,407 --> 00:04:56,210
its job, and it is exactly what you are protecting yourself for, every time you

84
00:04:56,210 --> 00:04:58,764
follow this method instead of skipping it.

85
00:04:58,764 --> 00:05:03,871
Notice also the shape of the repair: remove first, understand, then re-add properly.

86
00:05:03,871 --> 00:05:08,857
She did not patch the live problem in place, she took the capability out entirely,

87
00:05:08,857 --> 00:05:13,782
which cost her nothing but a few redundant drafts for a week, and brought it back

88
00:05:13,782 --> 00:05:17,247
only behind a label-scoped credential with C08 filled in.

89
00:05:17,247 --> 00:05:22,172
That is Section 6.4's promise kept: the reply-checking tool, added properly, is a

90
00:05:22,172 --> 00:05:25,760
genuinely good addition, and the chaser runs with it today.

91
00:05:25,760 --> 00:05:27,644
The idea was never the mistake.

92
00:05:27,644 --> 00:05:28,678
The Thursday was.
